---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CreateCouncilForm from '../components/CreateCouncilForm';
import WebMCPPanel from '../components/WebMCPPanel';
---

<Layout>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Adversarial AI · Live WebMCP evidence · Human-in-the-loop</p>
    <h1>The Council of Agents</h1>
    <p class="tagline">
      Four adversarial AI experts — <strong>Tech</strong>, <strong>Finance</strong>,
      <strong>Risk</strong> and <strong>Strategy</strong> — debate your hardest problem using
      <em>live, cited web evidence</em> instead of confident guesses. You hold the gavel.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-notes">
      <span>🔎 every claim must be sourced</span>
      <span>💥 no consensus-fawning — positions are attacked</span>
      <span>🛑 the debate pauses until you arbitrate</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="split">
    <CreateCouncilForm client:load />

    <aside class="card how-card" id="how">
      <h3>How a session unfolds</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Convene.</strong> State the problem, pick the experts and the number of debate
          rounds.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Investigate.</strong> Each agent designs its own web searches, fetches pages and
          writes <em>Zod-validated findings</em> with citations onto the shared blackboard.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Debate.</strong> Round by round, every agent must defend its stance and attack the
          others — the architecture <em>forces</em> disagreement out of the models.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Arbitrate.</strong> The engine pauses and waits for you. Inject a directive —
          e.g. <em>“Tech, verify direct payment to the venue organizer”</em> — and round two runs
          with your instruction in context.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Verdict.</strong> The council synthesizes a recommendation set, top risks and
          every source consulted — one validated JSON contract.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="muted">
        A single AI tends to agree with you. A council of isolated, adversarial agents with a
        mandatory citation discipline cannot — that is the anti-bias core of SynthCouncil.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <WebMCPPanel client:load />

  <section class="card tech-card">
    <h3>⚡ Built for the OpenAI WebMCP hackathon</h3>
    <div class="tech-grid">
      <div>
        <h4>WebMCP native</h4>
        <p class="muted">
          The web client registers <code>document.modelContext.registerTool</code> so the browser's
          model can convene, run, read and arbitrate councils itself.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>OpenRouter-powered</h4>
        <p class="muted">
          The engine speaks OpenAI-compatible chat completions to OpenRouter (Groq by default,
          Claude/GPT/others available) — no vendor lock-in.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Blackboard + DAG</h4>
        <p class="muted">
          Agents never talk directly. An orchestrator reads the shared blackboard and distributes
          the floor — stable, replayable, auditable.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Zod-locked contracts</h4>
        <p class="muted">
          Every model output is parsed and validated by shared Zod schemas; a hallucinated JSON
          shape can never crash the debate.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
